{"subjectId":"dsa-basics","id":"sorting","title":"Sorting","group":"","order":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Sorting is the process of arranging data in a specific order (increasing or decreasing). This topic integrates Bubble Sort, Selection Sort, Insertion Sort, Merge Sort, Quick Sort, and Heap Sort.</p><div>\n<p>sort()</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Works only for list</li>\n<li>modifies the smallest</li>\n<li>Sorts in place</li>\n<li>Use Tim Sort Algorithm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>sorted()</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Works for any iterable</li>\n<li>Does not modify the passes container, instead returns a list of sorted items</li>\n</ul>\n<p>NOTE : Both use Tim Sort Algorithm and Both are Stable</p>\n<h3>Tim Sort Algorithm</h3>\n<p>It's a hybrid algorithm that uses 'Merge Sort' and 'Insertion Sort; internally</p>\n<p>for sort() &amp; sorted() it uses O(nlog(n)) time</p>\n<h3>Sort()</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># List Sort()\n\nl1 = [5, 10, 15, 1]\nl1.sort()\nprint(l1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>l2 = [5, 10, 15, 1]\nl2.sort(reverse = True)\nprint(l2)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>l3 = ['Thrishank', 'Nani', 'Kuntimaddi']\nl3.sort()\nprint(l3)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def myfun(s):\n  return len(s)\n\nl4 = ['Thrishank', 'Nani', 'Kuntimaddi']\nl4.sort(key = myfun)\nprint(l4)\n\nl4.sort(key = myfun, reverse = True)\nprint(l4)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recap for class concept\n\nclass Point:\n  def __init__(self, x, y):\n    self.x = x\n    self.y = y\n\ndef myFun(p):\n  return p.x\n\nl = [Point(1, 15), Point(10, 15), Point(3, 8)]\n# print(l)\nl.sort(key = myFun)\nfor i in l:\n  print(i.x, i.y)\n\nprint()\n\nprint(l) # Returns a list of Objects</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Point:\n  def __init__(self, x, y):\n    self.x = x\n    self.y = y\n\n  def __lt__(self, other):   # Less than Operation\n    return self.x &lt; other.x\n\nl = [Point(1, 15), Point(10, 15), Point(3, 8)]\nl.sort()\nfor i in l:\n  print(i.x, i.y)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># If both are equal then we compare y values\n\nclass Point:\n  def __init__(self, x, y):\n    self.x = x\n    self.y = y\n\n  def __lt__(self, other):   # Less than Operation\n    if self.x == other.x:\n      return self.y &lt; other.y\n    else:\n      return self.x &lt; other.x\n\nl = [Point(1, 15), Point(10, 15), Point(3, 8)]\nl.sort()\nfor i in l:\n  print(i.x, i.y)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Sorted()</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Works for any Iterable (List, Tuple, Dictionary, Strings, Set)</li>\n<li>Returns a list with sorted items</li>\n<li>Parameters like reverse and key work same way as sort()</li>\n</ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>l = [30, 8, 23, 76, 12, 1, 12]\nls = sorted(l)\nprint(ls)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>l = [10, -15, -2, 1]\nls = sorted(l, key = abs)\nprint(ls)\n\nprint()\n\nls = sorted(l, key = abs, reverse = True)\nprint(ls)\n\nprint()\n\nls = sorted(l)\nprint(ls)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>t = (10, 12, 5, 1)\nprint(sorted(t))\n\nprint()\n\ns = {'Thrishank', 'Nani', 'Kuntimaddi'}\nprint(sorted(s))\n\nprint()\n\ns = 'gfg'\nprint(sorted(s))\n\nprint()\n\nd = { 10 : 'Thrishank', 12 : 'Nani', 5 : 'Kuntimaddi'}\nprint(sorted(d))\n\nprint()\n\nl = [(10, 15), (12, 15), (3, 8)]\nprint(sorted(l))</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Stability in Sorting Algorithms</h3>\n<p>arr[] = [ ('Anil', 50), ('Ayan', 80), ('Pyush', 50), ('Ramesh', 80) ]</p>\n<p>If two items have same value, then they should appear in the same order as they appeared in the original array is called stability.</p>\n<p>stable output : [ ('Anil', 50), ('Pyush', 50), ('Ayan', 80), ('Ramesh', 80) ]</p>\n<p>unstable output : [ ('Pyush', 50), ('Anil', 50), ('Ayan', 80), ('Ramesh', 80) ]</p>\n<p>Stable Sorts</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bubble Sort</li>\n<li>Insertion Sort</li>\n<li>Merge Sort</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Unstable Sorts</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Selection Sort</li>\n<li>Quick Sort</li>\n<li>Heap Sort</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Bubble Sort</h3>\n<p>Applications - Generally preferred in academics</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Approach\n\n'''\n1. Compare each pair of adjacent items in the list.\n\n2. Swap them if the first item is larger than the second.\n\n3. Repeat until you pass through the list without any swaps.\n\n4. The list is sorted when no more swaps are needed.\n\n'''\n\n\ndef bubbleSort(l):\n  n = len(l)\n  for i in range(n):\n    for j in range(n - i - 1):\n      if l[j] &gt; l[j + 1]:\n        l[j], l[j + 1] = l[j + 1], l[j]\n\nl = [10, 12, 5, 1, 10, 4, 56, 100]\nbubbleSort(l)\nprint(l)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Optimized Approach\n\ndef bubblesort(l):\n  n = len(l)\n  for i in range(n-1):\n    swapped = False\n    for j in range(n - i - 1):\n      if l[j] &gt; l[j + 1]:\n        l[j], l[j + 1] = l[j + 1], l[j]\n        swapped = True\n    if swapped == False:\n      return\n\nl = [10, 12, 5, 1, 10, 4, 56, 100]\nbubblesort(l)\nprint(l)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Selection Sort</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Theta(n^2)</li>\n<li>Does less memory writer comapred to Quick sort, Merge Sort, Insertion Sort. But Cycle sort is optimal in terms of memory</li>\n<li>Basic idea for Heap Sort</li>\n<li>Not Stable</li>\n<li>In Place sort : No need for extra array or space</li>\n</ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Approach\n\ndef selectionSort(l):\n  n = len(l)\n\n  for i in range(n):\n    min_index = i\n    for j in range(i+1, n):\n      if l[j] &lt; l[min_index]:\n        min_index = j\n    l[i], l[min_index] = l[min_index], l[i]\n\nl = [10, 12, 5, 1, 10, 4, 56, 100]\nselectionSort(l)\nprint(l)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Insertion Sort</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>O(n^2) : Worst Case</li>\n<li>In-place and stable</li>\n<li>used in practice for small arrays (Tim Sort &amp; Intro Sort)</li>\n<li>O(n) : Best Case</li>\n</ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Approach\n\ndef insertionSort(l):\n  for i in range(1, len(l)):\n    x = l[i]\n    j = i - 1\n    while j &gt;= 0 and x &lt; l[i]:\n      l[j + 1] = l[j]\n      j -= 1\n    l[j + 1] = x\n\nl = [10, 12, 5, 1, 10, 4, 56, 100]\ninsertionSort(l)\nprint(l)\n\n#                   O(n^2) : Worst Case</code></pre></figure></div><div>\n<ul>\n<li>Divide and Conquer Algorithm (Divide, Conquer, Merge)</li>\n<li>Stable</li>\n<li>Time complexity : Theta(n*log(n))</li>\n<li>Auxiliary Space : O(n)</li>\n<li>well suited for LinkedList that works in O(1) Auxiliary Space</li>\n<li>Used in external Sorting</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Merge Sort for Two Sorted Arrays</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> a = [10, 15, 20] ; b = [5, 6, 6, 30]<br><strong>Output:</strong> res = [5, 6, 6, 10, 15, 20, 30]</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Solution\n\ndef merge(a, b):\n  res = a + b\n  res.sort()\n  return res\n\na = [10, 15, 20]\nb = [5, 6, 6, 30]\nprint(merge(a, b))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient Solution\n\ndef mergeList(a, b):\n  res = []\n  m = len(a)\n  n = len(b)\n  i = 0\n  j = 0\n\n  while i &lt; m and j &lt; n:\n    if a[i] &lt; b[j]:\n      res.append(a[i])\n      i += 1\n    else:\n      res.append(b[j])\n      j += 1\n\n  while i &lt; m:\n    res.append(a[i])\n    i += 1\n\n  while j &lt; n:\n    res.append(b[j])\n    j += 1\n\n  return res\n\nl = [10, 15, 20]\nr = [5, 6, 6, 30]\nprint(mergeList(l, r))</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Merge Subarray</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong></p>\n</div>\n<p>a = [10, 15, 20, 11, 13]</p>\n<p>low = 0</p>\n<p>high = 4</p>\n<p>mid = 2</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Output:</strong></p>\n</div>\n<p>a = [10, 11, 13, 15, 20]</p>\n<p>so that the idea is split main array into two parts then merge them</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def merge(a, low, mid, high):\n  left = a[low : mid + 1]\n  right = a[mid + 1 : high + 1]\n  i = 0\n  j = 0\n  k = low\n\n  while i &lt; len(left) and j &lt; len(right):\n    if left[i] &lt; right[j]:\n      a[k] = left[i]\n      k += 1\n      i += 1\n    else:\n      a[k] = right[j]\n      j += 1\n      k += 1\n\n  while i &lt; len(left):\n    a[k] = left[i]\n    i += 1\n    k += 1\n\n  while j &lt; len(right):\n    a[k] = right[j]\n    j += 1\n    k += 1\n\na = [10, 15, 20, 11, 13]\nmerge(a, 0, 2, 4)\nprint(a)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Merge Sort Algorithm</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def mergeSort(arr, l, r):\n  if r &gt; l:\n    mid = (l + r) // 2\n    mergeSort(arr, l, mid)\n    mergeSort(arr, mid + 1, r)\n    merge(arr, l, mid, r)\n\narr = [10, 15, 20, 11, 13]\nmergeSort(arr, 0, 4)\nprint(arr)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Union of two sorted array</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong></p>\n</div>\n<p>a = [3, 5, 8]</p>\n<p>b = [2, 8, 9, 10, 15]</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Output:</strong> 2 3 5 8 9 10 15</p>\n</div>\n<h3>Naive Solution</h3>\n<p>1. Concatenate both lists</p>\n<p>2. Sort the concatenated list</p>\n<p>3. Print Distinct Elements</p>\n<p>Time Complexity : O((m + n) * log(m + n))</p>\n<p>m - len of a</p>\n<p>n - len of b</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Approach\n\ndef Print(a, b):\n  c = a + b\n  c.sort()\n  for i in range(0, len(c)):\n    if i == 0 or c[i] != c[i - 1]:\n      print(c[i], end = \" \")\n\na = [3, 5, 8, 9, 10, 15]\nb = [2, 8, 9, 10, 15]\nPrint(a, b)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient Approach\n\n# First, How does the Idea Works..?\n'''\na = [_, _, _, i, _, _, _, _, _]\nb = [_ ,_, _, _, j, _, _, _, _]\n\nif a[i] == a[i-1]:\n  i = i + 1\n\nif b[j] == b[j-1]:\n  j = j + 1\n\nif a[i] &lt; b[j]:\n  print(a[i], end = \" \")\n  i = i + 1\n\nif a[i] &gt; b[j]:\n  print(b[j], end = \" \")\n  j = j + 1\n\nif a[i] == b[j]:\n  print(a[i], end = \" \")\n  i = i + 1\n  j = j + 1\n\n'''\n\ndef PrintUnion(a, b):\n  i = 0\n  j = 0\n  while i &lt; len(a) and j &lt; len(b):\n    if i &gt; 0 and a[i] == a[i - 1]:\n      i = i + 1\n    elif j &gt; 0 and b[j] == b[j-1]:\n      j = j + 1\n    elif a[i] &lt; b[j]:\n      print(a[i], end = \" \")\n      i = i + 1\n    elif a[i] &gt; b[j]:\n      print(b[j], end = \" \")\n      j = j + 1\n    else:                          # a[i] == b[j]\n      print(a[i], end = \" \")\n      i = i + 1\n      j = j + 1\n\n  while i &lt; len(a):\n      if i &gt; 0 and a[i] == a[i-1]:\n        print(a[i], end = \" \")\n        i = i + 1\n\n  while j &lt; len(b):\n      if j &gt; 0 and b[j] == b[j-1]:\n        print(b[j], end = \" \")\n        j = j + 1\n\na = [3, 5, 8, 9, 10, 15]\nb = [2, 8, 9, 10, 15]\nPrintUnion(a, b)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Intersection of Two sorted Array</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong></p>\n</div>\n<p>a[] = { 3, 5, 10, 10, 10, 10, 15, 15, 20 }</p>\n<p>b[] = { 5, 10, 10, 15, 30 }</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Output:</strong> 5, 10, 15</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive\n\ndef intersection(a, b, m, n):\n  for i in range(m):\n    if i &gt; 0 and a[i-1] == a[i]:\n      continue\n    else:\n      for j in range(n):\n        if a[i] == b[j]:\n          print(a[i], end = \" \")\n          break\n\na = [3, 5, 10, 10, 10, 10, 15, 15, 20 ]\nb = [ 5, 10, 10, 15, 30 ]\n\nintersection(a, b, 9, 5)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient\n\n# First, How does the idea works..?\n'''\na = [_, _, _, i, _, _, _, _, _]\nb = [_ ,_, _, _, j, _, _, _, _]\n\nif i &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; a[i] == a[i-1]:\n  i += 1\n  continue\n\nif a[i] &lt; b[j]:\n  i += 1\n\nif a[i] &gt; b[j]:\n  j += 1\n\nif a[i] == b[j]:\n  print(a[i], end = \" \")\n  i += 1\n  j += 1\n\n'''\n\ndef intersection(a, b, m, n):\n  i = 0\n  j = 0\n\n  while i &lt; m and j &lt; n:\n    if i &gt; 0 and a[i-1] == a[i]:  # Don't consider common elements in Set A\n      i += 1\n      continue\n    elif a[i] &lt; b[j]: # Moves i value based on j value\n      i += 1\n    elif a[i] &gt; b[j]: # Moves j value based on i value\n      j += 1\n    elif a[i] == b[j]:\n      print(a[i], end = \" \")\n      i += 1\n      j += 1\n\na = [3, 5, 10, 10, 10, 10, 15, 15, 20 ]\nb = [ 5, 10, 10, 15, 30 ]\nintersection(a, b, len(a), len(b))</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Count inversions in Array</h3>\n<p>Inversions : A pair arr[i], arr[j] forms an inversion when ( i &lt; j ) and arr[i] &gt; arr[j]</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p>I/P = [2, 4, 1, 3, 5]<br>O/P = 3</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive\n\ndef inversion(arr):\n  n = len(arr)\n  res = 0\n  for i in range(n-1):\n    for j in range(i+1, n):\n      if arr[i] &gt; arr[j]:\n        res += 1\n  return res\n\narr = [2, 4, 1, 3, 5, 2]\nprint(inversion(arr))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient\n'''\n\narr[] = [_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _]\n\n         l                 m  m+1                        r\n\nm = (l+r)/2\n\nEvery Inversion(x, y) where x &gt; y has three possibilities\n\na) Both x &amp; y lie in the first half\nb) Both x &amp; y lie in the second half\nc) x lies in the first half and y lies in the second half\n\n'''\n\ndef countInv(arr, l, r):\n  res = 0\n  if l &lt; r:\n    mid = (l + r) // 2\n    res += countInv(arr, l, mid)\n    res += countInv(arr, mid+1, r)\n    res += countMerge(arr, l, mid, r)\n  return res\n\ndef countMerge(arr, l, mid, r):\n  left = arr[l : mid+1]\n  right = arr[mid+1 : r+1]\n  res, i, j, k = 0, 0, 0, l\n  while i &lt; len(left) and j &lt; len(right):\n    if left[i] &lt;= right[j]:\n      arr[k] = left[i]\n      i += 1\n    else:\n      arr[k] = right[j]\n      j += 1\n      res += (len(left) - i)\n    k += 1\n\n  while i &lt; len(left):\n    arr[k] = left[i]\n    i += 1\n    k += 1\n\n  while j &lt; len(right):\n    arr[k] = right[j]\n    j += 1\n    k += 1\n\n  return res\n\narr = [2, 4, 1, 3, 5]\nprint(countInv(arr, 0, 4))</code></pre></figure></div><div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> l = [3, 8, 6, 12, 10, 7]</p>\n</div>\n<p>p = 5</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Output:</strong></p>\n</div>\n<p>l = [3, 6, 7, 8, 12, 10]</p>\n<p>or</p>\n<p>l = [6, 3, 7, 12, 8, 10]</p>\n<p>............</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Solution\n\ndef partition(arr, p):\n  n = len(arr)\n  arr[p], arr[n-1] = arr[n-1], arr[p]\n  temp = []\n\n  for x in arr:\n    if x &lt;= arr[n-1]:\n      temp.append(x)\n\n  for x in arr:\n    if x &gt; arr[n-1]:\n      temp.append(x)\n\n  for i in range(len(temp)):\n    arr[i] = temp[i]\n\n  return temp\n\nl = [3, 8, 6, 12, 10, 7]\np = 3                        # Index Value to swap with last element\nprint(partition(l, p))</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Lomuto Parition (Guarantee)</h3>\n<p>arr = [10, 80, 30, 90, 40, 50, 70]</p>\n<p> l = 0 h = 6</p>\n<p>pivot = 70 # Last Element</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def lomutoPartition(arr, l, h):\n  pivot = arr[h]\n  i = l - 1\n\n  for j in range(l, h):\n    if arr[j] &lt;= pivot:\n      i += 1\n      arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]\n\n  arr[i+1], arr[h] = arr[h], arr[i+1]\n  return i+1\n\narr = [10, 80, 30, 90, 40, 50, 70]\nprint(arr)\nn = len(arr)\nprint(lomutoPartition(arr, 0, n-1))\nprint(arr)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Hoare's Partition (No Guarantee)</h3>\n<p>rr = [10, 80, 30, 90, 40, 50, 70]</p>\n<p> l = 0 h = 6</p>\n<p>after partition, pivot can be anywhere in leftside</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def hoarePartition(arr, l, h):\n  pivot = arr[l]\n  i = l - 1\n  j = h + 1\n\n  while True:\n    i += 1\n    while arr[i] &lt; pivot:\n      i += 1\n    j = j - 1\n    while arr[j] &gt; pivot:\n      j -= 1\n    if i &gt;= j:\n      return j\n    arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]\n\narr = [10, 80, 30, 90, 40, 50, 70, 100, 120, 10, 5]\nprint(arr)\nn = len(arr)\nprint(hoarePartition(arr, 0, n-1))\nprint(arr)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Quick Sort</h3>\n<p>1. Divide and Conquer Algorithm</p>\n<p>2. Worst Time Case : O(n^2)</p>\n<p>3. Despite O(n^2) worst cae, it is considered faster because of the following</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In-Place</li>\n<li>cache friendly</li>\n<li>Average-case : O(n * logn)</li>\n<li>Tail Recursive</li>\n</ul>\n<p>4. Partition is key function (Naive, Lomuto, Hoarse)</p>\n<p> where Naive is stable and Lomuto, Hoarse are unstable</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Using Lomuto\n\ndef qsort(arr, l, h):\n  if l &lt; h:\n    p = lomutoPartition(arr, l, h)\n    qsort(arr, l, p-1)\n    qsort(arr, p+1, h)\n\narr = [10, 80, 30, 90, 40, 50, 70]\nprint(arr)\nn = len(arr)\nqsort(arr, 0, n-1)\nprint(arr)\n\n# Best case : Theta(log n)\n\n'''\nDry Run\n\n1. Based on the pivot value it sort the elements\n\n2. [3, 5, 6, 7, 1, 2, 4]\n\n  - pivot is 4\n\n  - Element on left side are less than 4\n\n  - Element on right side are greater than 4\n\n3. sorted array : [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]\n\n'''</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Using Hoarse\n\ndef qsort(arr, l, h):\n  if l &lt; h:\n    p = hoarePartition(arr, l, h)\n    qsort(arr, l, p)\n    qsort(arr, p+1, h)\n\narr = [10, 80, 30, 90, 40, 50, 70]\nprint(arr)\nn = len(arr)\nqsort(arr, 0, n-1)\nprint(arr)\n\n# Note : Hoare's Partition is three times faster than Lomuto Partition\n\n'''\n1. Firstly this the collection is completely divided into single cell\n\n2. Then it is conquered in a way they swapped in acesending order\n\n'''</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Analysis of QuickSort : Hoare's Partition</h3>\n<p>Best Case : Theta(n * log_2 n)</p>\n<p>Worst Case : Theta(n^2)</p>\n<p>Average Case : O(n * log n)</p>\n<p>Space Complexity : Theta(n)</p></div><div>\n<ul>\n<li>can be seen as an optimization over selection sort</li>\n<li>Two steps</li>\n<li>build a maxHeap</li>\n<li>Repeatedly swap root with last node, reduce heap size by 1, and heapify</li>\n<li>Time complexity : O(n * logn)</li>\n<li>Auxiliary Space : O(1)</li>\n<li>Not stable</li>\n<li>used in Hybrid sorting algorithms like Intro Sort</li>\n</ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Step 1 : Build a maxHeap\n\ndef maxheapify(arr, n, i):\n    largest = i\n    l = 2 * i + 1\n    r = 2 * i + 2\n\n    if l &lt; n and arr[i] &lt; arr[l]:\n        largest = l\n\n    if r &lt; n and arr[largest] &lt; arr[r]:\n        largest = r\n\n    if largest != i:\n        arr[i], arr[largest] = arr[largest], arr[i]\n        maxheapify(arr, n, largest)\n\ndef buildHeap(arr):                        # Build Heap Tree from List\n    n = len(arr)\n    for i in range(n//2, -1, -1):\n        maxheapify(arr, n, i)\n\narr = [50, 20, 10, 4, 15]\nbuildHeap(arr)\nprint(arr)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Heap Sort\n\ndef heapsprt(arr):\n    n = len(arr)\n    buildHeap(arr)\n\n    for i in range(n-1, 0, -1):\n        arr[i], arr[0] = arr[0], arr[i]\n        maxheapify(arr, i, 0)\n\narr = [50, 20, 10, 4, 15]\nheapsprt(arr)\nprint(arr)\n\n# Time Complexity ; O(n * logn)</code></pre></figure></div>"}